              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em derived loop space
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+loop+space+object"} free loop space object
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+geometry"} derived geometry
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            be an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-algebraic+theory"} (∞,1)-algebraic theory
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi T
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Alg
                    %mn ∞
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \subset T Alg_\infty^{op}
            an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-site"} (∞,1)-site
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Isbell+duality"} formal duals
            of
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn ∞
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi Sh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H} = (\infty,1) Sh(C)
            encodes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+geometry"} derived geometry
            modeled on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
          %p
            A
            %strong derived loop space
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+loop+space+object"} free loop space object
            in such
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
          %p
            More specifically, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            is an ordinary
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theory"} Lawvere theory
            = succeed "-theory," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %msub
                    %mi Alg
                    %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T Alg_{\infty}
            are its simplicial algebras. There is a canonical embedding
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %msup
                    %mi Alg
                    %mi op
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi T
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Alg
                    %mn ∞
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T Alg^{op} \hookrightarrow T Alg_\infty^{op}
            of the ordinary algebras into the
            = succeed "-algebras," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi T
                  %msup
                    %mi Alg
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \in T Alg^{op}
            as an object of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            %em derived loop space
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+loop+space+object"} free loop space object
            computed in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
          %p
            The point is that the derived loop space of an ordinary
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi T
                  %msup
                    %mi Alg
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \in T Alg^{op}
            in general is a significantly richer object than the free loop space object of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            as computed just in the underived
            = succeed "-topos" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi Sh
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %msup
                      %mi Alg
                      %mi op
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)Sh(T Alg^{op})
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is 0-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truncated"} truncated
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi Sh
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %msup
                      %mi Alg
                      %mi op
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)Sh(T Alg^{op})
          %h2#function_complexes_on_derived_loop_spaces_hochschild_homology Function complexes on derived loop spaces: Hochschild homology
          %p
            The function complex on the derived loop space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℒ
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{L}X
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hochschild+homology"} Hochschild homology
            complex of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(X)
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "Hochschild%20cohomology#HochschildChainComplex"} Hochschild cohomology – As function algebra on the derived loop space
          %p
            Also see
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+loop+space+object"} free loop space object
            for more information.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loop+space+object"}> loop space object
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+loop+space+object"} free loop space object
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loop+space"}> loop space
                    ,
                    = succeed "," do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+loop+space"} free loop space
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+loop+space"}> formal loop space
                    ,
                    %strong derived loop space
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+microlocalization"} derived microlocalization
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inertia+orbifold"} inertia orbifold
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/suspension+object"} suspension object
              %ul
                %li
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/suspension"} suspension
          %h2#references References
          %p
            The relevance of derived loop spaces was amplified in a series of articles by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Ben-Zvi"} David Ben-Zvi
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Nadler"} David Nadler
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Ben-Zvi"}> David Ben-Zvi
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Nadler"} David Nadler
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %em Loop Spaces and Langlands Parameters
                    (
                    %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0706.0322"}> arXiv:0706.0322
                    )
                %li
                  %p
                    %em Integral Transforms and Drinfeld Centers in Derived Algebraic Geometry
                    (
                    %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0805.0157"}> arXiv:0805.0157
                    )
                %li
                  %p
                    %em Loop Spaces and Connections
                    (
                    %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1002.3636"}> arXiv:1002.3636
                    )
                  %p
                    This article uses Toën’s theory of
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+algebras+on+%E2%88%9E-stacks"} function algebras on ∞-stacks
                    for showing that the function complex on a derived loop space
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi ℒ
                          %mi X
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{L}X
                    is under mild conditions the
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hochschild+homology"} Hochschild homology
                    complex of
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi X
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                    hence by
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hochschild-Kostant-Rosenberg+theorem"} Hochschild-Kostant-Rosenberg theorem
                    the collection of
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K%C3%A4hler+differential"} Kähler differential
                    forms on
                    = succeed "," do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi X
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi ℒ
                          %mi X
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{L}X
                    that are invariant under the canonical
                    = succeed "-" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %msup
                              %mi S
                              %mn 1
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
                    on
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi ℒ
                          %mi X
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{L}X
                    are the
                    %em closed
                    forms. This also gives a geometric interpretation of the old observation by
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxim+Kontsevich"} Maxim Kontsevich
                    and others, that the differential and grading on the de Rham complex may be understood as induced from automorphisms of the
                    = succeed "." do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/odd+line"} odd line
                %li
                  %p
                    %em Loop Spaces and Representations
                    (
                    %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1004.5120"}> arXiv:1004.5120
                    )
